<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sagunto</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saguntum, Murviedro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sagunto is a town in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, north of the city of Valencia. As Iberian and Roman Saguntum it was besieged and taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> in 219 BCE, an event that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>. Its medieval name Murviedro, from 'old walls', referred to the ruins on its hilltop; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a> took it in 1098, and the town resumed the name Sagunto in the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sagunto.jpg" alt="Sagunto" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pelayo2 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castillo04_Sagunto.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sagunto lies about 30 km north of the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, on the Costa de Valencia on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, in the fertile comarca of Camp de Morvedre. The municipality has three urban nuclei: the old town, Ciutat Vella, Grau Vell and Puerto de Sagunto, and over half its population lives in the coastal settlement of Puerto de Sagunto. The old town climbs the hill that carries the castle, above the plain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 5th century BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a> built a walled settlement on the hill, known as Arse; a stretch of cyclopean limestone slabs from the former Temple of Diana survives near the church of Santa Maria. The city traded with coastal colonies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and minted its own coins. By 219 BCE Saguntum was a large, prosperous town that sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> against Carthage, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> besieged it first; after eight months of stiff resistance it fell, and the siege triggered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> retook it seven years later, and in 214 BCE it became a municipium. They built a great circus and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> seating 8,000, and texts suggest an amphitheatre and about 50,000 inhabitants. Its Catholic patron saint was Bishop Sacerdos, who died about 560.</p><p>After the Arab conquest in the early 8th century the town was called Morvedre, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> muri veteres, &quot;ancient walls&quot;. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a> conquered it in 1098, but the Muslims soon recovered it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-i-of-aragon">James I of Aragon</a> took it in 1238. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a> a Spanish attempt to relieve the French siege of the castle failed at the Battle of Saguntum on 25 October 1811, and much of the Roman theatre was dismantled for stone to restore the walls.</p><p>A Jewish community lived in the town under Muslim rule; after the massacre of 1391 it was the only surviving community in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, and about 500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> left at the expulsion of 1492. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> industry grew up in the late 19th century, and the last steel oven closed in April 1984.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The remains of Sagunto Castle, with ramparts of Roman and Moorish origin, crown the hill. The Roman theatre on its northern slope, partly restored in the late 20th century, was the first official National Monument declared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, in 1896. The town also has the Gothic church of Santa Maria, the neoclassical Palau Municipal, one of the best-preserved Jewish quarters in Spain and a history museum in the Jewish quarter, whose largest collection is from the Ibero-Roman period.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sagunto” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Mediterranean coast and the Ebro valley had felt Greek, Phoenician and Iberian influence for centuries and became thoroughly Roman, with cities such as Tarraco, Barcino (Barcelona), Emporiae, Valentia, <b>Saguntum</b>, Carthago Nova and Caesaraugusta (Zaragoza).</blockquote><a href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Saguntum</b> had a large fortress by the 5th century BCE, and Castellet de Banyoles at Tivissa was one of the most important settlements of the north-east.</blockquote><a href="/culture/iberians">Iberians · Society</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Saguntum</b>, called Arse by its inhabitants, grew in the 5th century BCE on a long rocky hill above the Palancia river, near the coast north of Valencia, within the Edetanian world.</blockquote><a href="/state/saguntum">Saguntum</a></li><li><blockquote>After the collapse of the Taifa of Valencia's independence and the installation of al-Qadir as a Castilian client in 1086, the Valencian notable Abu Isa ibn Labbun made himself master of the fortress town of <b>Murviedro</b>, the ancient Saguntum, north of Valencia.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-murviedro">Taifa of Murviedro</a></li></ul></section>
